# Packages the stand needs, and why each one is here.
#
# The same idea as deployment.manifest, for the other half of the machine.
# That file made file placement reproducible; nothing did the same for
# dependencies, so the stand's requirements lived only in whoever set it up.
# smartmontools is what exposed the gap: the host keeps 215 GB of mirror, all
# of Forgejo and the whole release tree on one mechanical disk, and could not
# read that disk's health at all because nobody had noticed the package was
# absent.
#
# install-ci-host.sh installs what is missing; verify-deployment.sh reports it
# as drift. One source, read by both, so an installer that provides something
# and a verifier that expects something else cannot disagree.
#
# This is a list of what the stand REQUIRES, not an inventory of what it has.
# Adding a line is a statement that something here breaks without it.
#
# Columns: package name, then free text.

# smartmontools and nut left this list on 14 August 2026, when the stand moved
# off the physical N100 into a virtual machine.
#
# smartmontools was here because tank lived on one mechanical ada0 whose health
# nothing could read. The VM's tank is a virtual disk: SMART belongs to the
# hypervisor's host, and asking a guest for it returns nothing useful. nut read
# UPS status over the network, and the UPS now backs the hypervisor host, not
# the guest -- the guest is shut down by the host, which is what
# AutomaticStopAction is for.
#
# Both are still real requirements of the appliance itself; this file is only
# about the stand.
rsync           mirror synchronisation
sqlite3         the CI dashboard reads Forgejo's database
git             checkouts for builds
zstd            job logs are stored compressed
ca_root_nss     TLS trust for fetching sources and lists
